message:
{{#is_alert}}No metrics have arrived from switch-core for 15 minutes. Either the server is down, it can't reach the collector, or reporting was turned off.

**Check:** whether the pods are running and serving. If they are, the collector or the telemetry relay is the problem.

Every other alert goes quiet at the same time, so this one explains their silence.

Signal board: open "Switch signal" in Datadog's dashboard list.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: switch-core has stopped reporting.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}switch-core can't reach its database. Users can't do anything that reads or writes data.

**Check:** the database instance's status, and the pods' readiness.

Signal board: open "Switch signal" in Datadog's dashboard list.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: the database is unreachable.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}Requests timed out waiting for a database connection. Users see slow or failed actions.

**Check:** pool in use on the Signal board; a long query or a transaction left open usually holds the pool.

Signal board: open "Switch signal" in Datadog's dashboard list.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: the database is refusing connections.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}On pod {{pod_name.name}}, every reporting interval for the last 10 minutes peaked with the whole database pool in use, so it keeps running into overflow. Requests slow down next, then start timing out.

**Check:** what holds connections: a slow query, a transaction left open, or a burst of work. Raise the pool size only once you know.

Signal board: open "Switch signal" in Datadog's dashboard list.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: the database pool keeps hitting its limit.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}The listener that delivers room messages is down. Messages are saved but don't reach anyone until it comes back.

**Check:** the pods' logs for the message listener; a restart brings it back while you look for why.

Signal board: open "Switch signal" in Datadog's dashboard list.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: room messages are not being delivered.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}More than 5 deliveries failed in 10 minutes. Some people or agents are missing messages.

**Check:** the delivery errors in the logs; one bad room can't stop the others, so look for which rooms.

Signal board: open "Switch signal" in Datadog's dashboard list.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: messages are failing to deliver.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}More than 5 messages failed to save in 10 minutes. They are lost: the sender sees nothing, the room stays quiet.

**Check:** database health first, then the send errors in the logs; `kind` there says which sends failed.

Signal board: open "Switch signal" in Datadog's dashboard list.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: messages are failing to send.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}At least one configured messaging app bridge (Slack, Discord, Teams...) has stopped. People on that platform are cut off from their rooms; the rest of Switch works.

**Check:** switch.bridges.running by platform on the Signal board, and "Bridge ... crashed" in the logs.

Signal board: open "Switch signal" in Datadog's dashboard list.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: a messaging app bridge is down.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}The {{platform.name}} bridge "{{bridge_name.name}}" has crashed. People in that workspace are cut off from their rooms; the rest of Switch works.

**Check:** "Bridge ... crashed" in the logs for that bridge, then restart it or, if nobody uses it any more, deactivate it.

Signal board: open "Switch signal" in Datadog's dashboard list.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: the {{platform.name}} bridge "{{bridge_name.name}}" runs again.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}At least 10 requests failed with a server error in 10 minutes. Users see errors in the Console, Gateway or agents.

**Check:** server errors by route on the core service health dashboard, then that route's logs.

A count, not a percentage: at pilot's traffic one failure among a few requests would read as a high error rate.

Signal board: open "Switch signal" in Datadog's dashboard list.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: server errors are elevated.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}The slowest 5% of requests took over 2 seconds, for 10 minutes. Everything feels sluggish.

**Check:** the database pool and event-loop lag on the Signal board; slowness usually starts there.

Signal board: open "Switch signal" in Datadog's dashboard list.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: the API is slow.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}The slowest 5% of messages took over 10 seconds to reach their recipients, for 15 minutes. Conversations stall.

**Check:** event-loop lag and the database pool on the Signal board.

Signal board: open "Switch signal" in Datadog's dashboard list.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: messages are arriving late.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}A switch-core process has stalled for over a second at a time, for 10 minutes. Every room and bridge on that pod waits while it does.

**Check:** CPU on the pods and the slowest database queries; one blocking call stalls everything.

Signal board: open "Switch signal" in Datadog's dashboard list.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: the server is stalling.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}Fewer than a third as many agents are connected as at this time a week ago. Agents that lost their connection stop answering in their rooms.

**Check:** pod restarts and agent connection logs; a pod loss makes its agents reconnect elsewhere. If last week was the odd one out (an outage, or a load test that connected extra agents), this clears on its own.

Signal board: open "Switch signal" in Datadog's dashboard list.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: agent connections are dropping en masse.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_no_data}}The 09:00 report of switch-core hasn't posted to this channel for over 25 hours, so a quiet morning here doesn't mean all is well.

**Check:** the daily report job's last run in this deployment (its log says why), and that its settings are in place.{{/is_no_data}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: the morning report posted again.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_warning}}More than 5% of auto-route decisions in the last 15 minutes got no usable answer. Most messages still route, but about one untagged message in twenty reaches nobody and its room gets an "auto-route is unavailable" notice. A slow model provider (decisions timing out) shows up here first.

**Check:** the share of decisions that failed and timed out, then decision failures by reason. Dashboard: the decision routing section of "Switch — core service health".{{/is_warning}}{{#is_alert}}More than 20% of auto-route decisions in the last 15 minutes got no usable answer, counted against at least 10 decisions so that one or two failures in a quiet room don't fire. People's untagged messages reach nobody, or the triage agent, and rooms get an "auto-route is unavailable" notice.

**Check:** decision failures by reason and backend errors by kind (by model; `unknown` is a decision with no answer). `timeout` or `unreachable` on the server default points at the decision service; on `endpoint`, at one workspace's own endpoint. The decision record logged for each message names the room.

Dashboard: the decision routing section of "Switch — core service health".{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: decisions are failing.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}The server's default decision service has failed its health check for 5 minutes. Auto-route can't pick an agent in workspaces that use the server default, so their untagged messages reach nobody, or the triage agent. switch-core itself stays in service: this check never takes a pod out.

**Check:** the decision service's pod, not switch-core's: whether it runs, restarts or is ready, its logs, and the model server behind it.

Dashboard: the decision routing section of "Switch — core service health".{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: the default decision service is unhealthy.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}For 5 minutes the oldest message waiting for an auto-route decision has been over a minute old. People are waiting for an answer to start, and a message still waiting at the pending limit (two minutes by default) is dropped as expired.

**Check:** decision duration and failures: a slow or timing-out backend backs the queue up. If decisions look healthy, the decision sweep in switch-core's logs.

Dashboard: the decision routing section of "Switch — core service health".{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: undecided messages are backing up.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}At least one untagged message waited past the pending limit (two minutes by default) without a decision and was dropped. Each one is a question nobody answered.

**Check:** whether "undecided messages are backing up" or "the default decision service is unhealthy" fired too. If neither did, a switch-core restart with messages waiting is the usual cause. The decision record logged for each expired message names the room.

Dashboard: the decision routing section of "Switch — core service health".{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: decisions are expiring.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_warning}}The slowest 5% of auto-route decisions took over 1.5 seconds, for 30 minutes. People wait longer for an answer to start; nothing is lost. A warning only: decisions have a target of under a second, so this tells you without paging.

**Check:** decision duration beside model time, by model. If model time is slow too, the model or its host is; if only duration is, the network or the backend's queue.

Dashboard: the decision routing section of "Switch — core service health".{{/is_warning}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: decisions are slow.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}For over 5 minutes no pass of the WorkOS mirror has read the membership events through, or no process is running the mirror at all (no data for 15 minutes). People removed or demoted in your organization's account settings keep their access in Switch until it catches up.

**Check:** switch-core's logs for "WorkOS mirror": WorkOS answering with errors, the API key refused, or one event failing on every pass (it is passed over after 5 tries). With no data, whether any switch-core pod is up and holds the `workos_mirror` lease.

Dashboard: the WorkOS directory section of "Switch — core service health".{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: WorkOS membership changes are not reaching Switch.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}An organization has waited over 15 minutes in the WorkOS mirror's full recheck: a change for it failed and checking it against WorkOS keeps failing. Its members in Switch may not match your organization's account settings, including people who should have been removed.

**Check:** switch-core's logs for "Checking organization" and "Applying WorkOS's membership", which name the organization and the error.

Dashboard: the WorkOS directory section of "Switch — core service health".{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: an organization is stuck in the WorkOS recheck.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}The WorkOS mirror hasn't finished checking every linked organization in full for over 26 hours. That check is the backstop for changes WorkOS sends no event for and for members with no WorkOS identity.

**Check:** switch-core's logs for "daily check": a failure reading the linked organizations, or a mirror restarting before it gets through.

Dashboard: the WorkOS directory section of "Switch — core service health".{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: the daily WorkOS check hasn't finished.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}WorkOS deleted an organization that a Switch organization follows. Switch removed nobody: the Switch organization keeps its members, workspaces, rooms and agents, its admins see an "organization deleted" notice, and the mirror no longer checks it.

**Check:** switch-core's logs for "was deleted; its members are left as they are", which names both organizations. If the customer is leaving, delete the Switch organization with `switch-purge-organization`, named by that WorkOS organization's id (docs/old/workos-directory.md, "When a customer leaves"). Otherwise, find out who deleted it in WorkOS: changes to that organization's members no longer reach Switch.

Dashboard: the WorkOS directory section of "Switch — core service health".{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: a WorkOS organization that Switch follows was deleted.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}A request to switch-core raised an error nothing handled. Whoever sent it (Console, an agent, a bridge) got a 500 and whatever they were doing did not happen.

**Check:** the log sample below has the stack trace; Error Tracking groups repeats of the same one. A burst right after a deploy points at the release.

Signal board: open "Switch signal" in Datadog's dashboard list, section "Digging into error logs".{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: requests are failing with unhandled errors.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}A switch-core process stopped renewing its lease without shutting down (a pod shutting down in a deploy does not fire this), so the agents connected through it no longer count as connected. Their people see them go offline until they reconnect to another pod.

**Check:** whether a pod restarted or was killed (OOM, node drain) at that time, and the database pool on the Saturation panels: a process that cannot reach the database cannot renew.

Signal board: open "Switch signal" in Datadog's dashboard list, section "Digging into error logs".{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: a switch-core pod lost its hold on its agents.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}switch-core kept failing to post messages to Slack, Discord or another messaging app for 10 minutes. People there do not see what agents and others wrote in Switch.

**Check:** the log names the bridge; then its platform's status page, and whether the bridge's token was revoked. One message retrying every 5 s for under a minute stays below this limit.

Signal board: open "Switch signal" in Datadog's dashboard list, section "Digging into error logs".{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: messages are not reaching a messaging app.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}switch-core logged more than 25 errors in 15 minutes, far above its usual handful. Something that is not covered by the other alerts is failing.

**Check:** the pattern list on the signal board's "Digging into error logs" section shows which message it is.

Signal board: open "Switch signal" in Datadog's dashboard list, section "Digging into error logs".{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: switch-core is logging a burst of errors.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}Agent controllers opened more than 900 connections in 30 minutes: the reconnect storm getting worse. Pilot runs at about 570 day to day because of the reconnect loop we know of; lower this to 450 once that is fixed. While it lasts, Console can show agents as Connecting and messages to them wait for the next connection.

**Check:** `event` and `reason` on `switch.controllers.connections` say how connections open and end (taken over by another pod, heartbeat lapsed); the controller log on an affected machine says what it saw. A rollout reconnects every controller once, which stays well below this limit.

Signal board: open "Switch signal" in Datadog's dashboard list.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: agent controllers keep reconnecting.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}For about 10 minutes, {{value}} cloud machine(s) ({{backend.name}}) should be running but have sent no status report.

**Check:** whether those machines are running in the cloud account, and whether their controllers can reach Core. One machine's silence is a claim to check, not proof of an outage.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: every expected cloud machine ({{backend.name}}) is reporting again.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}No Core pod has heard from the hosted controller service (the reconciler that starts and stops cloud machines) for over 10 minutes. New machines won't start and stopped ones won't wake.

**Check:** the controller service's pods and their logs, and that its token still matches Core's hosted controller config.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: the hosted controller service is calling Core again.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}{{value}} bearer credentials were refused as invalid or missing on the agent, controller and hosted controller routes in 10 minutes. That's where a guessed token lands, so this looks like someone trying tokens.

**Check:** the refusals by reason and route on the dashboard, and Core's logs for where they come from. Token exchange and enrolment refusals have their own low-urgency alert.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: bearer credential refusals are back to normal.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}{{value}} controller token exchanges, enrolments or in-cluster worker credentials were refused in 10 minutes. A revoked or replaced controller stops after one refused exchange, so a few are normal (one per controller revoked); this many means a large revoke (such as a migration batch), an in-cluster worker with an old credential after a rotation, or someone trying credentials or enrolment codes.

**Check:** the refusals by reason and route on the dashboard, whether many controllers were revoked just now, and whether the provisioner's and operator's credentials were just rotated.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: controller token exchanges and enrolments are back to normal.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}{{value}} password sign-ins failed in 10 minutes, for a wrong password or an unknown email. A rise may be someone guessing passwords.

**Check:** Core's logs and the gateway's access logs for where the attempts come from. Where SSO is enough, turning password sign-in off stops it.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: password sign-in failures are back to normal.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}{{value}} hosted machine launches failed in 30 minutes.

**Check:** the failures by reason on the dashboard (not scheduled, never started, never connected, a wake that didn't come back, a launch refused before it was ever ready), then the controller service's logs for the cloud errors.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: hosted machine launches are succeeding again.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}The hosted controller service saw {{value}} cloud calls fail in 15 minutes (capacity shortages aside). Machines may not start, stop or wake.

**Check:** the failures by call and kind on the dashboard. `permission` points at the service's cloud role, `throttled` at too many calls, `other` at the reconciler's own logs.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: cloud calls for hosted machines are working again.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}{{value}} cloud calls failed for lack of capacity in 15 minutes: the cloud had no room for the instance type, or the installation reached its machine limit. New and woken machines wait until there's room.

**Check:** the failures by call on the dashboard, and the installation's machine limit and instance types.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: hosted machines are getting capacity again.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}The hosted controller service reported {{value}} errors in 15 minutes while it kept running.

**Check:** the errors by kind on the dashboard: `cloud` (its cloud calls), `core_api` (calling Core), `state` (its own state store) or `other`, then the service's own logs.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: the hosted controller service's errors are back to normal.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}For 30 minutes, new cloud machines have taken over 10 minutes (p95) from being requested to their first report.

**Check:** the cloud account's capacity and the hosted controller service's logs.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: cloud machines are starting in normal time.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}For 15 minutes, a cloud machine's memory has been over 90% full ({{value}}%). Its agents may slow down or be stopped.

**Check:** the memory panel on the dashboard, and which agents run on the fullest machines.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: cloud machine memory is below 90%.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}For 15 minutes, a cloud machine's disk has been over 90% full ({{value}}%). Agents on it may fail to write.

**Check:** the disk panel on the dashboard.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: cloud machine disks are below 90%.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}The hosted metrics sampler failed {{value}} times in 15 minutes, so the cloud machine gauges may be stale or missing.

**Check:** Core's database connection and its logs (the sampler's own warnings aren't exported).{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: the hosted metrics sampler is working again.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}{{value}} exceptions nothing in Core handled in 10 minutes.

**Check:** the counts by component and type on the dashboard, then the `runtime.unhandled_exception` log lines and the full stack uvicorn or asyncio logged beside each.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: unhandled exceptions are back to normal.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}{{value}} gateway requests were answered 503 in 10 minutes because a pod already ran as many as its share of the database pool allows. People see "Switch is busy right now" in Switch Console and the web app.

**Check:** the pool and the slowest routes on the dashboard: one slow query holding turns, or simply more load than the pods carry. Add a pod before raising `GATEWAY_POOL_SHARE`, which takes connections from the pod's own work.

Signal board: open "Switch signal" in Datadog's dashboard list.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: the gateway is no longer turning people away.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}{{value}} gateway requests were answered 429 in 30 minutes because their caller already had `GATEWAY_REQUESTS_PER_CALLER` running. The rest of the server is unaffected; that caller is waiting.

**Check:** Core's logs name the caller once a minute ("Turned away a request to ... from ..."). A client sending many requests at once usually wants one batched request instead, as Switch Console's sidebar got in #1983. Raise the limit only if the requests can't be batched.

Signal board: open "Switch signal" in Datadog's dashboard list.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: no caller is hitting its share of the gateway any more.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}Agent {{[@switch.agent_id].name}} posted {{value}} messages in 5 minutes, more than 60 a minute on average. In the load tests' busiest realistic step an agent posts about once every 3 minutes, so this is a loop, a runaway session, or someone flooding rooms with the agent's key.

**Check:** the log sample for which rooms (`switch.room_id`), and the agent's sessions in the gateway. Two agents answering each other in a loop both show up here.

**To stop it:** suspend the agent or person, which ends their access at once and keeps everything for review: the `switch-suspend` CLI on a switch-core pod, or the gateway's suspend and freeze routes (for an agent, a person, or a whole workspace). Lift the suspension the same way once it is cleared. See the suspend runbook in the operator docs.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: agent {{[@switch.agent_id].name}} is posting at a normal rate again.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}Agent {{[@switch.agent_id].name}} read the history of {{value}} different rooms in 10 minutes. An agent normally reads the few rooms it works in; sweeping through many is what collecting a workspace's conversations looks like.

**Check:** the log sample for the rooms (`switch.room_id`; `security_details.connected` is false for a room read without connecting to it), what the agent was asked to do, and who owns it. `security.agent.rooms_listed` lines show it listing rooms beforehand.

**To stop it:** suspend the agent or person, which ends their access at once and keeps everything for review: the `switch-suspend` CLI on a switch-core pod, or the gateway's suspend and freeze routes (for an agent, a person, or a whole workspace). Lift the suspension the same way once it is cleared. See the suspend runbook in the operator docs.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: agent {{[@switch.agent_id].name}} is reading a normal number of rooms again.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}Person {{[@switch.actor_user_id].name}} (or agent {{[@security_details.acting_agent_id].name}}, when an agent did it) added {{value}} members to rooms in 10 minutes. Adding people or agents to rooms in bulk is a way to read or post where they shouldn't.

**Check:** the log sample for the rooms (`switch.room_id`) and who was added (`security_details.client_type`, `security_details.agent_id`, `security_details.user_id`), then the workspace audit log. A room template setting up a team is a legitimate burst.

**To stop it:** suspend the agent or person, which ends their access at once and keeps everything for review: the `switch-suspend` CLI on a switch-core pod, or the gateway's suspend and freeze routes (for an agent, a person, or a whole workspace). Lift the suspension the same way once it is cleared. See the suspend runbook in the operator docs.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: {{[@switch.actor_user_id].name}} is adding room members at a normal rate again.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}Agent {{[@switch.agent_id].name}} or person {{[@switch.actor_user_id].name}} uploaded or downloaded {{value}} files in 10 minutes. Bulk file transfer is how data leaves a workspace.

**Check:** the log sample for the direction (`switch.event`), the rooms and the sizes (`security_details.size`). Switch Console fetches a room's images each time it shows them, so a person scrolling a room full of images can reach this too.

**To stop it:** suspend the agent or person, which ends their access at once and keeps everything for review: the `switch-suspend` CLI on a switch-core pod, or the gateway's suspend and freeze routes (for an agent, a person, or a whole workspace). Lift the suspension the same way once it is cleared. See the suspend runbook in the operator docs.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: file transfers by {{[@switch.agent_id].name}} / {{[@switch.actor_user_id].name}} are back to normal.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}Agent {{[@switch.agent_id].name}} was issued {{value}} service tokens (GitHub and other connected services) in 10 minutes. A token lasts up to an hour and is meant to be cached, so this many means a client that doesn't cache, or someone harvesting tokens with the agent's credentials.

**Check:** the log sample for the service and who asked (`security_details.service`, `security_details.principal`, `security_details.controller_id`), and the service's own audit log for what the tokens were used for.

**To stop it:** suspend the agent or person, which ends their access at once and keeps everything for review: the `switch-suspend` CLI on a switch-core pod, or the gateway's suspend and freeze routes (for an agent, a person, or a whole workspace). Lift the suspension the same way once it is cleared. See the suspend runbook in the operator docs.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: service tokens for agent {{[@switch.agent_id].name}} are back to a normal rate.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

{{#is_alert}}Switch Trust blocked or redacted a message from agent {{[@switch.agent_id].name}} or person {{[@switch.actor_user_id].name}}: it matched the workspace's policy (secrets, personal data, or whatever else the policy names).

**Check:** the log sample for the policy, the room and each finding's category and detector (`security_details.*` from the workspace audit row, never the text itself). One is usually a mistake; several from one sender, or an agent that keeps trying, is worth a closer look.

**To stop it:** suspend the agent or person, which ends their access at once and keeps everything for review: the `switch-suspend` CLI on a switch-core pod, or the gateway's suspend and freeze routes (for an agent, a person, or a whole workspace). Lift the suspension the same way once it is cleared. See the suspend runbook in the operator docs.{{/is_alert}}{{#is_recovery}}Recovered: no new blocked or redacted messages from {{[@switch.agent_id].name}} / {{[@switch.actor_user_id].name}} in 5 minutes.{{/is_recovery}}

@REPLACE-WITH-NOTIFICATION-HANDLE

